slang: covergroup built-in method names do not hide user names

A covergroup's body, its coverpoints and crosses hold the built-in methods start, stop,
get_coverage, ...; slang let those hide user names:
- a sample() argument (or covergroup argument) named start or stop, which is in the scope
  above the body: `coverpoint start` bound to the built-in function ("'void' is not a valid
  type for a coverage expression");
- a bin named start or stop in a coverpoint ("redefinition of 'start'"), and
  binsof(cp.start) then found the method ("binsof target is not a coverpoint or coverpoint
  bin");
- an implicit coverpoint of a cross item named start ("redefinition", and binsof(start)
  failed).
Now Lookup::unqualifiedImpl skips a built-in of a covergroup body when the covergroup has
an argument of that name; Scope::handleNameConflict lets a user member take a built-in's
name in a coverage scope; an implicit cross coverpoint whose name is taken is kept out of
the name map (Scope gives it its name back after inserting it); and
ConditionBinsSelectExpr::fromSyntax finds an implicit coverpoint among its cross's targets
first. Found in OpenTitan's i2c DV (all 26 of its errors).

